        "overviewText": "### Jaisalmer: A Fort Where People Still Live\n\nJaisalmer is a city built from yellow sandstone. It sits at the edge of the Thar Desert and turns gold at sunset.\n\nOur Jaisalmer group trip from Ahmedabad runs for 3 days and 2 nights. You board an overnight AC bus or Tempo Traveller and wake up in the Golden City. We take care of your stay, breakfasts, dinners, sightseeing and a free camel safari. Our trip leader stays with the group from start to finish.\n\n### Places to Visit in Jaisalmer\n\n- **[Jaisalmer Fort](https://indiatour.live/attractions/jaisalmer-fort) and city sightseeing.** We walk through one of the few living forts in India. Patwon Ki Haveli and Gadisar Lake are on the list too.\n- **[Bada Bagh](https://indiatour.live/attractions/bada-bagh).** We stop at the royal cenotaphs on the hill.\n- **[Kuldhara](https://indiatour.live/attractions/kuldhara-ghost-village).** This village was abandoned overnight in the early 1800s. We explore the ruins on foot.\n- **[Sam Sand Dunes](https://indiatour.live/attractions/sam-sand-dunes).** This is the highlight. You ride a camel, watch the sunset and end the night at a bonfire.\n- **[Longewala](https://indiatour.live/attractions/longewala-war-memorial) and [Tanot Mata Mandir](https://indiatour.live/attractions/tanot-mata-temple).** We visit two border sites tied to the 1965 and 1971 wars.\n\n### Things to Do in Jaisalmer\n\n- **Ride a camel at Sam.** This one is on us. The safari is free with your trip.\n- **Catch sunset on the dunes.** Get there early and find a spot on the ridge. The light changes every few minutes.\n- **Sit around the bonfire.** The desert is dark and quiet at night. Look up. You will see more stars than you do in the city.\n- **Get lost in the fort lanes.** Shops, cafes and guesthouses sit inside the fort walls. Go slow. Look at the carved stone balconies.\n- **Shop the bazaars.** Look for mirror work, block prints, leather goods and silver jewelry. Bargain politely.\n- **Eat local.** Try dal baati churma, ker sangri and gatte ki sabzi. Lunch is not included, so this is a good excuse.\n- **Try dune bashing or a jeep ride.** This is not part of the package. We point you to vetted operators at Sam. The cost is yours.\n- **Take photos at golden hour.** The fort, Bada Bagh and the dunes all look best in early morning or late afternoon.\n\n### Travel Tips for Jaisalmer\n\n- **Pack layers.** Desert nights drop fast in winter. Carry a jacket, a scarf and socks.\n- **Protect yourself from the sun.** Carry sunscreen, sunglasses and a cap. The sun is strong even in winter.\n- **Carry a scarf or dupatta.** It keeps the dust and wind off your face at the dunes.\n- **Wear comfortable shoes.** The fort, Kuldhara and Bada Bagh are all on foot. Sand and stone do not go well with flip-flops.\n- **Keep cash handy.** Lunch is not included. Some small shops near the sights do not take UPI reliably.\n- **Carry your ID.** Border areas like Longewala and Tanot Mata Mandir need it for permits.\n- **Follow the rules at border sites.** Photography can be restricted in some areas. Your trip leader will tell you where.\n- **Carry water and snacks for the bus.** The overnight ride is long. Motion sickness tablets help if you need them.\n- **Charge your phone and power bank.** Power at desert camps can be patchy.\n- **Permits and transport:** If you are not traveling with India Tour, you need to arrange these yourself. If you are traveling with India Tour, we handle them.",
